{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass at the time when the flock conceived</b> — this was after he [Laban] had changed his wages, and therefore he said, \"for I have seen all that Laban is doing to you.\"" "And the sense of <b>and behold, the he-goats mounting the flock</b> is that He showed him in a dream that the he-goats would mount the flock and all of them were streaked, and afterward they were all speckled, and then they were all spotted. And He informed him that he was seeing the injustice that Laban was doing to him by changing his wages, and that He would make the offspring born according to the appearance that Jacob needed. From this point onward Jacob would not make rods, for he trusts in HaShem and is exalted (Proverbs 29:25)." "<b>And behold, the he-goats mounting the flock</b> — the bucks and rams are called עַתּוּדִים, for every large one among the flock is called thus, even among great men: \"all the he-goats of the earth\" (Isaiah 14:9). And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 31:10): even though Laban had separated them all so that the flock would not be bred like them, the angels were bringing them from the flock handed over to Laban's sons to the flock that was in Jacob's hand. And according to the plain sense, this was a vision to assure him that they would produce such offspring. And the proof is from the word וְהִנֵּה (\"and behold\"), for in every dream this is said (above 28:12–13; below 40:9, 16), in the sense as though the matter is before his eyes. And in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 74:3) they did not mention the angels but only as a midrash — they said: it is not written עוֹלִים (\"mounting\") here but הָעֹלִים (\"the ones mounting\"), i.e., those known and designated for this. But the plain sense of Scripture is as we have said."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:37:42.375921697Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}